    "role": "Three native King blooms transform the same optical court and require low, high and returning reflections.",
    "solution": "Carry shade through the western shaft and park it beyond the light. Open the low receiving face and turn the starting mirror so the first sun travels through the portal behind the King. Climb the eastern approaches and strike his unlit side. His first bloom lifts the central casting platform into a wall. Climb that wall to the high mirror, readdress the portal to the upper face and return the second sun. Strike the King again; the same wall now clears a lower underpass. Return beneath it to the starting mirror, open the floor mouth, and cut the mirror from its other side to send the last sun downward into that mouth. Retreat beneath that beam to the original low portal wall, climb its western recovery shelf, and jump-dash back above the light. Re-enter the court through the changed lower route and take the last opening. His actual defeat extinguishes the light and opens the stable lower departure beneath his former attack lane.",
    "interconnection": "One starting mirror works in two orientations; one platform becomes first the route upward and then the roof of the lower return. All three optical decisions expose the same native King from behind, whose blooms physically change the court. The original low receiving wall becomes the safe western relaunch after the final mirror cut.",
    "novelty": "Boss progress manufactures the useful wall for the next optical address, then removes its lower obstruction for the return to the original mirror.",
    "nearestExisting": "B44 brings shade through a return; B49 changes floor roles; B50 treats a portal as movable architecture. Native Sunspire uses changing windows in compact arenas. This turns its three native blooms into one vertical optical return around a shared casting wall.",
